1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,600
Booking confirmed. But the saved phone is wrong. An
AI fix needs more than a convincing answer.

2
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:16,340
AccessFinish connects a proposed code change to your
review, approval of that exact patch, and
independent execution evidence.

3
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:29,880
For our second case, we deliberately seeded a phone
serialization fault. One new NVIDIA Nemotron 3 Super
proposal, through Nebius Token Factory, repaired it
with an operator-supplied diagnosis.

4
00:00:30,060 --> 00:00:40,220
The entered phone was saved exactly, including
leading zeroes, and reopened in a fresh browser. A
different phone also passed the regression control.

5
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:49,120
A hardcoded reference repair passed the first
booking, then failed the different-phone check. Both
failures and successes remain visible.

6
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:59,380
We compared AccessFinish with a conventional
test-only workflow using the same build, browser,
database reader, reopen, and regression checks.

7
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:13,080
Across six paired variants and twelve isolated
executions, both workflows agreed on every
correctness outcome. The new Nemotron proposal also
passed both workflows in two additional replays.

8
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:25,900
The difference is the repair decision. AccessFinish
rejected missing and stale approvals, then attached
each accepted approval to its exact source, patch,
and run.

9
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:32,720
The original keyboard date case remains separate:
two complete passes from five guided model attempts.

10
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:43,620
These are two exposed cases in one application,
including one deliberately seeded fault. They do not
establish general repair accuracy or time savings.

11
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:48,280
AccessFinish. Review the change. Know which repair
you can accept.
